in our series the new normal we look at  how the pandemic is dramatically  changing our everyday lives  much like the airlines america's  passenger railroad  amtrak is facing the worst economic  disaster  in its history an unprecedented drop in  demand is forcing service cuts  and furloughs chris van cleave spoke to  amtrak's new ceo  and took a ride to see what's changed  all aboard doesn't amount to much these  days the pandemic has flattened amtrak  ridership  on our quick half hour trip from  washington to baltimore  joshua bailey was one of the handful on  board it hasn't been packed at all  so that's a good thing amtrak is capping  ridership at 50 percent for distancing  and requiring masks for all on board  plexiglas barriers have been added in  the cafe car and in the stations  the first thing you're going to notice  there aren't that many people on board  this is a five-car train two of them are  almost completely empty in fact amtrak  says ridership  is down in the range of 85 percent  the biggest change you probably won't  see is amtrak's new cleaning protocol  the trains are disinfected at the end of  trips they have air filters that  circulate cabin air  40 to 50 times an hour completely  replacing the air in a car  every four to five minutes touch  surfaces are wiped down before departure  and during the trip we have now focused  more so on making sure  handles seats tables bathrooms contact  points  are all cleaned thoroughly like the  trains the stations are quiet largely  empty  many of the businesses at washington's  union station are closed and seating  areas have been removed to encourage  distance this was supposed to be the  year amtrak  broke even for the first time in its 49  year history  but by the time new ceo bill flynn  officially started in april  ridership was in free fall down 97  percent  did you have a moment where you were  like what did i get myself into no i  never had that  what did i get myself into a moment i  mean the situation that  where it's happening here at amtrak it's  happening across our country  how concerned are you that some of your  customers don't come back  i think that's a question everyone in  transportation is asking themselves  today  amtrak has already reduced service and  will cut most long-haul trains from  daily to a few trips per week in october  that's causing pushback from 16 senators  who sign letters criticizing the move  montana steve daines writing it's  irrevocably hurting hundreds of  communities in small towns already  devastated by the covet 19 pandemic  i want to be clear we're fully committed  to our long distance service  amtrak now expects next year's ridership  to be cut  in half forcing the furlough of 20  percent of its workforce by october  it's part of an effort to slash costs by  500 million dollars  while asking the federal government  which subsidizes the railroad  for more than 3.5 billion to weather the  pandemic next year  very difficult decision the ultimate  decision is to size  the company as best we can we're  operating at about sixty percent  of our normal capacity against about  fifteen percent level of riderships  our intention is to restore services we  certainly want to bring  people back  flynn expects many of these new cleaning  protocols to be part of  a lasting new normal amtrak has required  masks  since may they say passenger compliance  has been pretty good but  if somebody refuses to wear a mask they  can be denied boarding  if they're not wearing it on the train  they can be told to get off at the next  stop  anthony chris van cleave reporting  taking a ride on amtrak for us thanks  chris  you also have some changes for airline  mass policies chris what is the latest  on that  sure this week we have seen four major  u.s carriers tighten their mass policies  delta on monday  said if you don't want to wear a mask  you have to speak to a medical  professional  at the airport to receive an exemption  otherwise you've got to wear it united  american and southwest yesterday  said anybody above the age of two has to  wear a mask united american  says you need to wear the mask at the  airports as well as on the flight  if you don't comply you can be banned  from flying that airline  throughout the remainder of the pandemic  anthony yeah rules on mass tightening  all over chris thank you very much
 

